---
interface Props {
  protocol: "tcp" | "udp";
  port: number;
}
const { protocol, port } = Astro.props;
---

<section class="lab-section" aria-labelledby="live-capture">
  <div class="section-heading">
    <h2 id="live-capture">Capture your own loopback traffic</h2>
    <p>
      Use traffic from a practice program on your computer. Have the program
      ready before starting the capture.
    </p>
  </div>
  <ol class="lab-steps">
    <li>
      <strong>Select the loopback interface.</strong> In Capture → Options, look
      for
      <code>lo</code> on Linux, <code>lo0</code> on macOS, or the Npcap loopback
      capture interface on Windows. The Wi-Fi interface does not show transfers
      whose two endpoints use <code>127.0.0.1</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Start before sending.</strong> Set the optional capture filter
      <code>{protocol + " port " + port}</code> to record both directions on the
      lab port. Captures without this filter may include other loopback traffic.
      <slot />
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Stop, inspect, and save.</strong> Use the display filter
      <code>{protocol + ".port == " + port}</code> in the main window. Select a
      row, expand the transport header in Packet Details, and compare its fields
      with the highlighted Packet Bytes. Save the capture as a{" "}
      <code>.pcapng</code>.
    </li>
  </ol>
  <div class="note">
    <h3>Capture filter or display filter?</h3>
    <p>
      A capture filter decides which packets get recorded. A display filter
      changes which recorded packets you can see. Clear the display filter to
      see everything that was captured.
    </p>
  </div>
  <p>
    <a href="https://www.wireshark.org/docs/wsug_html_chunked/ChCapCaptureFilterSection.html">
      Wireshark capture filter guide
    </a>
    ·
    <a href="https://wiki.wireshark.org/CaptureSetup/loopback">
      Platform loopback capture notes
    </a>
  </p>
</section>
